\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Drag-and-drop interactions for composing chart blocks. (A) Dropping a block into another block's plotting region creates layering by sharing both spatial dimensions. (B) Dropping along a boundary creates concatenation; the boundary determines the shared dimension and block order. The same interaction extends to polar coordinates through angular and radial boundaries. (C) Dropping onto an internal mark or structural element creates nesting, replacing or overlaying repeated elements while inheriting their data and spatial references. \relax }}{5}{figure.caption.17}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:drag-drop}{{3}{5}{Drag-and-drop interactions for composing chart blocks. (A) Dropping a block into another block's plotting region creates layering by sharing both spatial dimensions. (B) Dropping along a boundary creates concatenation; the boundary determines the shared dimension and block order. The same interaction extends to polar coordinates through angular and radial boundaries. (C) Dropping onto an internal mark or structural element creates nesting, replacing or overlaying repeated elements while inheriting their data and spatial references. \relax }{figure.caption.17}{}}
\newlabel{fig:drag-drop@cref}{{[figure][3][]3}{[1][4][]5}}
